# Payment Providers (PSP) Architecture — ChartVolt

## 📐 The Big Picture

ChartVolt was designed for **multi-PSP from day one**. The architecture has two layers:

1. **PSP-agnostic spine** — generic models, services, and abstractions that key off a free-string `provider` field.
2. **PSP-specific implementations** — concrete adapters for each gateway (Nuvei is the primary one; Stripe and Paddle are partially integrated alongside).

Currently active gateways:

| Provider | Deposits | Auto-withdrawals | Saved cards (UPO) | Chargebacks | Status |
|---|---|---|---|---|---|
| **Nuvei** | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | ✅ | Primary, full-featured |
| **Stripe** | ✅ (partial) | ➖ | ➖ | ⚠️ adapter needed | Deposit path exists |
| **Paddle** | ✅ (stub) | ➖ | ➖ | ➖ | Skeleton only |

## 🟢 What is Already PSP-Agnostic (Do Not Re-implement Per PSP)

Treat these as **shared infrastructure** — they accept any `provider` string. Adding a new PSP must NOT fork or duplicate them.

| Component | File | Why it's agnostic |
|---|---|---|
| Generic provider table | `database/models/payment-provider.model.ts` | `slug` + `credentials[]` + `webhookUrl` + `testMode` + `processingFee` for any PSP |
| Wallet transaction provider tag | `database/models/trading/wallet-transaction.model.ts` (`provider` field, indexed) | Free string; reports/queues filter on it |
| Chargeback ingestion | `lib/services/security/chargeback-handler.ts` | Module doc explicitly says "PSP-agnostic: callers (Nuvei / Stripe / Paddle / future providers) pass a normalized input here" |
| Chargeback case lifecycle | `lib/services/security/chargeback-case.service.ts` + `chargeback-case.writers.ts` | Stores `provider` string only; transitions are PSP-independent |
| Fraud payment fingerprint | `lib/services/fraud/payment-fraud.service.ts` | `paymentProvider: "stripe" \| "paypal" \| "nuvei" \| "custom" \| string` |
| Withdrawal validator gates (KYC, caps, cooldown, hold period, 2FA, allowed methods, etc.) | `lib/services/withdrawal-validator.service.ts` | `PayoutCategory` enum already includes `stripe_payout`, `stripe_refund`; every gate is PSP-agnostic |
| Provider availability API | `app/api/payment-config/route.ts` | Already enumerates Stripe/Paddle/Nuvei to the client |
| Reconciliation | `lib/services/platform-financials.service.ts` + `PlatformTransaction` model | Keys on `provider` + `sourceType`, not hard-coded names |
| Security alerts / audit log / restrictions / live-ops dashboard | All services | Read `provider` string from `WalletTransaction` |
| Front-end provider switcher | `components/trading/DepositModal.tsx` (`type PaymentProvider = "stripe" \| "paddle" \| "nuvei"`) | Selection UI already exists |

**Rule:** when touching any of the above, keep them PSP-agnostic. Never add `if (provider === "nuvei")` branches in the spine — push provider-specific behavior into adapters.

## 🔴 What is Tightly Coupled to Nuvei (Do Not Change Without a Plan)

These files contain Nuvei-specific knowledge. Adding a peer PSP means writing **parallel** files for the new gateway, NOT modifying these.

| Layer | File(s) | What it does |
|---|---|---|
| SDK wrapper | `lib/services/nuvei.service.ts` (~2143 lines) | open-order, payment-status, withdrawal, refund, account-capture, UPO management |
| Webhook (DMN) | `app/api/nuvei/webhook/route.ts` (~1648 lines) | `advanceResponseChecksum` HMAC verify, deposit + withdrawal lifecycle, idempotent claim |
| Auto-withdrawal | `app/api/nuvei/withdrawal/route.ts` (~1097 lines) | UPO refund → original-method → bank payout fallback chain |
| Saved-card store | `database/models/nuvei-user-payment-option.model.ts` | UPO IDs needed for automated card refunds |
| Account capture (KYC bank account) | `app/api/nuvei/account-capture*` + `app/payment/nuvei/page.tsx` | Nuvei iframe flow |
| Withdrawal settings flag | `database/models/withdrawal-settings.model.ts` (`nuveiWithdrawalEnabled`) | Single boolean — needs to generalize to a per-provider map when adding peers |
| Front-end deposit/withdrawal flows | `components/trading/DepositModal.tsx`, `WithdrawalModal.tsx`, `components/wallet/BankAccountsSection.tsx` | Nuvei SDK iframe / popup / mobile redirect logic |
| Chargeback evidence enrichment | `lib/services/security/chargeback-evidence.service.ts` (line ~95) | Reaches into `NuveiUserPaymentOption` for card brand/last4 — needs PSP-aware switch when adding peers |
| Chargeback lookup | `lib/services/security/chargeback-lookup.service.ts` | Resolves UPO from `createdFromTransactionId` — Nuvei-only enrichment |
| Attack-test simulator | `lib/services/simulator/attack-tests/{scenarios,webhook-crafter}.ts` | Nuvei DMN replay format for fraud regression |

## 📊 Effort & Risk Matrix (Adding ONE Peer PSP, Full Parity)

These are realistic estimates for one experienced full-stack dev who already knows the codebase. They assume the new PSP runs **alongside** Nuvei (not replacing it).

| Domain | Coupling | Effort | Risk | Notes |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| Deposit front-end (provider switcher) | low | 1–2d | low | Already exists; just wire the new option |
| Deposit back-end open-order | low | 1–3d | low | Stripe path exists as template |
| **Deposit webhook (HMAC + lifecycle)** | high | 3–5d | **high** | Each PSP has its own signature scheme; idempotency + race conditions |
| Withdrawal validator gates | low | 0d | low | Already PSP-agnostic |
| **Auto-withdrawal logic** | high | 5–10d | **high** | UPO/card-refund equivalents per PSP; fallback chain |
| **Saved-card refunds (UPO equiv)** | high | 6–10d | **high** | New Mongo model + KYC capture flow + chargeback evidence integration |
| Chargeback ingestion | low | 1d | medium | `chargeback-handler.ts` is agnostic — just add a webhook adapter that calls it |
| Chargeback evidence | low | 1–2d | low | Replace `NuveiUserPaymentOption` lookup with a PSP-aware switch |
| Fraud (fingerprint, decline velocity, alerts) | low | 0–1d | low | Already PSP-agnostic |
| Admin UI (settings, queues, dashboards) | medium | 3–5d | low | UI surfaces are provider-aware via the `provider` string |
| Reconciliation | low | 0d | low | Already keys on `provider` + `sourceType` |
| Attack-test simulator | medium | 2–4d | low | Build a parallel webhook-crafter for the new signature scheme |

**Realistic totals**

- Full parity (deposits + auto-withdrawals + saved cards + chargebacks): **~25–45 dev-days**.
- Deposits + chargebacks only (no auto-withdrawals, no saved cards): **~10–15 dev-days**.

## ⚠️ Real Dangers (Plan Around These)

1. **Saved-card portability is impossible.** Nuvei UPOs are bound to the Nuvei merchant; you can't move them to another PSP. If a peer PSP needs to refund a Nuvei-originated deposit, that refund still has to go through Nuvei. Plan to keep Nuvei active for the long tail of refunds (~6 months of chargeback window).
2. **Don't replace, augment.** Replacing Nuvei (vs. adding a peer) is dangerous because in-flight chargebacks (90+ days post-deposit) reference `nuveiUserPaymentOption.createdFromTransactionId` and the evidence packet. Migrating those would require a read-only Nuvei adapter for ~6 months while old chargebacks settle.
3. **Webhook signature is the unforgiving piece.** Each PSP has its own scheme:
   - Nuvei: `advanceResponseChecksum = SHA256(secret + totalAmount + currency + timestamp + PPP_TransactionID + Status + productId)`
   - Stripe: `Stripe-Signature` header HMAC-SHA-256 of `timestamp.payload`
   - Paddle: HMAC-SHA-256 of raw body with `Paddle-Signature`
   Get this wrong once and you've shipped an unsigned webhook = forgeable deposits.
4. **Withdrawal automation is fragile per PSP.** Nuvei has a 5-step fallback (UPO refund → original method → bank payout → manual). Stripe Refund API caps at 90 days for some networks. PayPal Payouts needs separate API permissions. This is real engineering, not configuration.
5. **`nuveiWithdrawalEnabled` boolean** must be generalized to `autoWithdrawalEnabledByProvider: { nuvei: true, <new>: true }` before adding a second auto-withdrawing PSP. This is a schema migration, not code-only.
6. **`WalletTransaction.provider` is a hot index.** It powers the live-ops dashboard, financial reports, fraud filters, reconciliation, and chargeback queue. Never change its semantics; only ever **add** new string values.

## ✅ Pattern: How to Add a Peer PSP Safely

Follow this order. Each step is independent and shippable.

### Phase 1 — Deposits + Chargebacks (lowest risk, highest value)

1. **Register the provider** in the `PaymentProvider` collection (`slug`, `displayName`, `credentials`, `testMode`, `webhookUrl`).
2. **Create the SDK wrapper** at `lib/services/<psp>.service.ts`. Mirror the structure of `lib/services/nuvei.service.ts` (class-based, exposes `getCredentials`, `openOrder`, `getPaymentStatus`, `isEnabled`, `getClientConfig`).
3. **Create the open-order route** at `app/api/<psp>/open-order/route.ts`. Mirror `app/api/nuvei/open-order/route.ts`:
   - Auth + per-user + per-IP rate limiting
   - Sanitize amounts
   - Look up user country / VAT applicability
   - Pre-create a `WalletTransaction(status="pending", provider="<psp>", metadata.userAgent, metadata.clientIp, metadata.client{Country,City,Region})`
   - Return the PSP's session token / checkout URL to the client
4. **Create the webhook route** at `app/api/<psp>/webhook/route.ts`. Mirror `app/api/nuvei/webhook/route.ts`:
   - **Signature verify FIRST** (per the PSP's scheme)
   - On signature failure: `reportWebhookSignatureFailure(...)` + `recordSecurityAlert(...)` + return 200 (never 4xx — attackers learn from rejections)
   - **Idempotent claim** with `findOneAndUpdate({ status: { $in: ["pending", "awaiting_payment"] } }, { $set: { status: "processing" } })` so concurrent DMNs don't double-credit
   - On approve: extract authentication facts (AVS / CVV2 / 3DS / authCode / bin / issuer country) into `metadata` — the chargeback defense packet reads these
   - On decline/error: `recordDecline(userId)` + `recordDecline("ip:" + ip)` for card-testing detection
   - On chargeback: call `handleChargeback(...)` from `lib/services/security/chargeback-handler.ts` with normalized input
5. **Wire the front-end** in `components/trading/DepositModal.tsx`:
   - Add the new slug to `type PaymentProvider`
   - Add a card to the provider grid using the existing layout
   - Lazy-load the PSP's SDK script
6. **Add the chargeback adapter** — a thin function that converts the PSP's chargeback webhook payload to `HandleChargebackInput` and calls `handleChargeback(...)`. The chargeback case, restriction, evidence packet, defense flow, and reconciliation all just work.

### Phase 2 — Auto-withdrawals (only if needed)

7. **Generalize the withdrawal-settings flag** from `nuveiWithdrawalEnabled: boolean` to a per-provider map. Schema migration required.
8. **Build the auto-withdrawal route** at `app/api/<psp>/withdrawal/route.ts`. Mirror `app/api/nuvei/withdrawal/route.ts` but use the new PSP's refund/payout APIs. **Always call `validateWithdrawal(...)`** from `lib/services/withdrawal-validator.service.ts` — never reimplement gate logic.

### Phase 3 — Saved cards / KYC capture / refunds (only if needed)

9. **Create a sibling UPO model** at `database/models/<psp>-user-payment-option.model.ts`. Do **NOT** reuse `nuvei-user-payment-option.model.ts` — keep PSP UPO stores isolated.
10. **Update the chargeback evidence builder** in `lib/services/security/chargeback-evidence.service.ts` to look up the right UPO model based on `chargeback.provider`.
11. **Build the account-capture flow** at `app/api/<psp>/account-capture*` and the matching front-end iframe page.

### Phase 4 — Hardening (mandatory before going live)

12. **Build a parallel attack-test suite** mirroring `lib/services/simulator/attack-tests/scenarios.ts` for the new PSP's webhook (signature replay, missing signature, malformed payload, double-credit attempts, prototype-pollution keys).
13. **Run the existing attack suite** — none of the existing Nuvei attack tests should regress.
14. **Verify reconciliation** — make a test deposit, refund, and chargeback through the new PSP and confirm `PlatformTransaction` entries balance against the PSP statement.

## 🚫 Anti-Patterns (Never Do These)

- ❌ Branch on `provider === "nuvei"` inside the agnostic spine. Push the difference into the PSP adapter instead.
- ❌ Reuse `nuvei-user-payment-option.model.ts` for another PSP. Create a sibling model.
- ❌ Skip signature verification "for testing" on the new PSP. Always run signature verify FIRST in the webhook handler — it's the contract.
- ❌ Process a deposit webhook synchronously. Use the existing pattern: claim with `findOneAndUpdate`, then process; this prevents double-credit on retries.
- ❌ Replace Nuvei wholesale. Run PSPs in parallel; toggle availability via `PaymentProvider.isActive`.
- ❌ Change `WalletTransaction.provider` semantics. Only ever **add** new values; never rename or repurpose.
- ❌ Reimplement withdrawal gates inside a new PSP's auto-withdrawal route. Always call `validateWithdrawal(...)` from the central validator.
- ❌ Return 4xx from a webhook on signature failure. Return 200 + audit log so attackers don't learn the validation logic.
- ❌ Skip the attack-test suite. The existing suite is the reason the Nuvei DMN endpoint is hardened — replicate it for the new PSP.

